{*
   aLatteX Demo: Extends, page - a database template that extends a file.

   Two levels: this record, and base.latte in /views/. That is the whole of it -
   the template stays where a CMS template belongs, editable in the manager, and
   the markup every page shares lives in one file under version control.

   Blocks are the interface between them. base.latte declares title, bodyClass,
   crumbs, content, aside and foot; this fills the ones it cares about and
   inherits the rest, so a page that needs nothing special says nothing.
*}
{extends 'base.latte'}

{block title}{$pagetitle} (extended){/block}

{block bodyClass}alx-base alx-page{/block}

{block content}
    <h1>{$pagetitle}</h1>

    <p>
        This template is a record in <code>site_templates</code>. Everything
        around it - the doctype, the header chunk, the footer - comes from
        <code>base.latte</code>, which it names with
        <code>&#123;extends 'base.latte'&#125;</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        EVO tags work in both halves. This one is in the child:
        <strong>[*pagetitle*]</strong>, and the setting in the layout's
        <code>&lt;title&gt;</code> is <strong>[(site_name)]</strong>. The page
        goes through parseDocumentSource() after Latte either way, so where a
        tag was written makes no difference to whether it resolves.
    </p>

    <p>Document content, below the template's own markup:</p>
    <div class="alx-content">[*content*]</div>
{/block}

{block aside}
    <p>Aside replaced by the child. base.latte's default is gone.</p>
{/block}
